# 6. Multiple Objects

This becomes clearer when multiple objects are created.

```java
class Student {

    String name;

    Student(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
}

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Student s1 = new Student("A");
        Student s2 = new Student("B");

        System.out.println(s1.name);
        System.out.println(s2.name);
    }
}
```

Conceptually:

```text
STACK                         HEAP

s1 ───────────────────────► Student
                              name = "A"

s2 ───────────────────────► Student
                              name = "B"
```

Each `new Student()` creates a **separate object**.

Therefore, `s1` and `s2` refer to different objects.

